[Music]  [Applause]  so I'm a roboticist and I get two types  of reactions from people when I talk  about AI and robotics some people get  really nervous ask about cyber Skynet  and then ask me when the robots will  take over their jobs and there is the  other group who gets very excited than  who says well when will I be able to  offload even more to machines now I  believe that everyone stands to benefit  from AI but we have to listen to the  fears of that first group we have to  provide solutions and we have to provide  different perspectives for how to see  things differently and that starts with  understanding that AI is just a tool and  like any other tool it isn't inherently  good or bad it is what we choose to do  with it and I happen to believe that we  can choose to make to do some incredible  things and let me start with an example  so this week I published a paper about a  new algorithm that has all sorts of  interesting theoretical properties and  essentially this algorithm matches  supply and demand and it can do so in  real-time for thousands of jobs we  implemented this algorithm on the  publicly available taxi data set for New  York City and we showed that 3000  capacity for cars that do ride-sharing  and trip pooling can meet the demand  that is currently serviced by 14,000  taxis in New York City they can meet 98%  of this demand within a guaranteed trip  delay of a maximum of three point five  minutes so the journal is smooth we  wanted to ask one question does this  mean that 10,000 jobs go away and my  perspective is that it really depends  how we look at this problem so we can  provide a new perspective with a new  business model where we say that if taxi  drivers agree to join the system in  other words they stop being independent  agents and they do what the system tells  them to do and customers agree  to share rides then we can get 10,000  cars of roads we can provide drivers  with shorter shifts for the same amount  of income and we achieve better traffic  lower pollution improve quality of life  for all plus we create new jobs because  getting these systems deployed  maintained and operated will create new  jobs but in the future taxes could  become autonomous and so this will  change the job landscape however we have  some time because level for autonomy is  actually not ready for private and I'm  sorry for public roads but level for  autonomy is ready for lower complexity  private roads like the one you see in  this video which was done in Singapore  so the technology for autonomous driving  in these lower types of complexity  environments could have immediate social  benefits for example this technology on  private waste could give Bob a much  higher quality of life in his retirement  say Bob wants to go to feed the fish  who's his friend Alice to do so Bob  brings up his you got wheels interface  fix the type of vehicle that suits him  today it may be a golf cart vehicle  tomorrow maybe a shopping cart and when  the robot arrives Bob gets a call he  heads out and then he's off together  with Alice to feed the fish  so although although this robot car has  no driver we should not consider that it  displaced his work because the system  does not exist at the moment however we  can imagine all the different jobs that  will be involved in the manufacturing  deploying maintaining and serving this  kind of new approach to autonomous  mobility all this while giving Bob a  much higher quality of life  so today personal mobility could include  systems like the golf cart I showed you  it  also include other types of vehicles  like these kinds of scooters wheelchairs  and many many other vehicles and you can  begin to imagine the applications that  would be enabled by such fleets now the  same technology for autonomous driving  can be mapped on to wearable devices to  provide safe navigation to blind and  visually impaired users which at the  moment does not exist at the moment in  today's technological age blind and  visually impaired users still walk  around with a walking stick so what can  we do well we can map the perception  system of a self-driving car onto a  laser belt and some cameras and with  this the system is able to identify the  world and even describe it in words that  get projected onto a Braille buckle for  the blind users so for instance this  technology could describe a fabulous  window display or the presence of an  obstacle behind or say hello to a friend  Alice who is passing by or hears a cat  so in fact this technology is really not  that far off and here is an example of  using this technology with our blind  user that is truly able to navigate the  world going up and down the stairs  moving safely say to the bus stop and  once the bus arrives moving to an empty  seat on the bus and at the moment this  is simply not possible and it's a huge  challenge for for blind people just  close your eyes and imagine what it  would take for you to find an empty seat  in this room without the use of your  perception system so these are some of  the amazing things that technology can  do to improve quality of life to improve  efficiency to provide better access by  taking advantage of our state-of-the-art  in autonomy for motion and in addition  to these great individual benefits  all of these systems are novel so  producing them will create new jobs now  in addition to autonomy in motion we  also see tremendous progress in how much  information machines can process and I  call this autonomy at rest and medicine  is a great example machines today can  look at more radiology scans than  radiologists would see in a lifetime and  in a recent study an AI system was asked  to look at lymph node cells and classify  them as cancerous or not cancerous the  AI system made 7.5 percent error as  compared to the human error of 3.5%  but when working together the human and  the Machine achieved 0.5% error much  better than each one separately so  machines working together with people  have tremendous ability to make an  impact  now today these systems are primarily  available in advanced cancer centers but  imagined deploying them everywhere  imagine giving doctors in rural areas  access to this kind of tool and then  augmenting with the latest and greatest  in natural language understanding that  will enable doctors to access the latest  clinical trials relevant to the patient  they're considering now even without  having studied those trials yet another  case study is for lawyers  so machines and humans working together  can be better lawyers and today we  already have seen a revolution in the  job of a lawyer  due to word processing internet and  email that have completely changed how  lawyers draft documents access  information and share information in the  next wave of tools in natural language  processing promised an equally profound  impact on the job of a lawyer with  natural language understanding systems  machines could predict a decision  machines could examine thousands of  pages of prior cases and provide the  most relevant information to lawyers but  machines cannot be lawyers because  machines will not be able to counsel a  client they will not be able to persuade  the judge and they will not be able to  write a compelling argument at least not  yet  so machines cannot be lawyers but they  can change the nature of jobs lawyers do  and the same principles apply to many  other professions for example teachers  today teachers spend 1/5 of their time  grading with the use of natural language  processing they can spend a little less  time grading and a little bit more time  with students now advances in autonomy  in motion and autonomy at rest also will  impact production and we all assume that  robots and AI will lead to fewer  manufacturing jobs but what if they lead  to better jobs and even more jobs  what if robots and software could allow  people very fine-grain control over what  manufacturing lines look like this is a  really challenging problem today  reconfiguring a manufacturing line takes  a very long time and for that reason  most manufacturing processes are for  most assembly lines are configured for  products that are in existence for years  but what if you put very quickly  reconfigure manufacturing lines well  then we we will really enable mass  customization so robots have already  enabled mass production and I believe  that robots plus computation will enable  mass customization and this could mean  this could meet the rising demand of  demikhov of customization and  personalization in just about everything  we buy and in fact today we do not have  the ability to keep up with this demand  at a price point that most people can  afford and because of that we are forced  to  use fixed prod products mostly offshore  and these products get distributed in  big shops but by teaming AI robots and  people together we can reshape the world  of manufacturing we can imagine a world  where experts design templated products  that get instantiated by people at home  and get produced locally in smaller  factories that are closer to population  centers this could revolutionize  production it could revolutionize  manufacturing it could bring a lot of  manufacturing jobs back so when you look  at all the positives of what AI can do  with deductive and they are driven  reasoning with personalization  customization the democratization for  predicting improving and adapting you  can begin to imagine a potential impact  in so many different industries but it's  worth noting that there aren't that  today's AI has significant limitations  and in fact in mark zuckerberg words  most of what ai systems do today are  variants of the same fundamental pattern  recognition techniques and when we  recognize an object we are not able to  go beyond that we are not able to  recognize actions and activities and  behaviors so there are significant  challenges and even with a major  advances being made today artificial  intelligence is nowhere near close to  people in perceiving reasoning  communicating and in creativity most of  the machine learning tools we have today  are one-off solutions that are only  usable by experts and I'm not saying  computer science experts it's machine  learning experts and then there are a  lot of important facts about the state  of technology so crunching large data  sets does not translate into knowledge  and making complex calculations does not  produce autonomy also the achieving  really high a really high  percentage of correctness 99.99% correct  which is what you need in self-driving  is exponentially harder than achieving  say 90% correct and perception and  action is much harder than perception  which is harder than crunching data now  all of this suggests that today's AI  limitations hold it back from out  sinking humanity and this table shows  four categories of jobs for rough  categories of jobs according to skills  and qualifications AI systems are not  ready for the first for the jobs that  fall in the first row and robots will  struggle with the jobs that fall in the  last row because a robot that can do  welding will be hopeless in forestry or  in construction so AI can achieve some  automation for the middle level jobs but  even the most vulnerable jobs that fall  in those middle categories like  accounting even these types of jobs  involve significant social interactions  that are difficult to automate so  altogether this suggests that we should  we should look at tasks not professions  and this chart shows you a graph that  was done by McKinsey here you see a  number of professions and in color you  see the technological readiness for the  different types of tasks people do in  those Patel profile professions so  people manage others they apply  expertise they do unpredictable or  predictable physical work and they do  data processing the targets for  automation are in green and in blue and  the size of the bubble shows how much  time a person spends doing that type of  task so it's kind of exciting to think  about AI making everyone's job better by  taking away the repetitive and  uninteresting aspects of the job but  it's much easier to think about which  tasks might be automated than to imagine  the new kinds of jobs that will be  created and so for instance in the 20th  century agricultural employment dropped  from 40 percent to 2 percent and perhaps  a farsighted specialist in the 19th  century could have predicted this but  only two decades ago we had the big  dot-com boom and nobody predicted all  these different types of jobs that now  provide so much employment so I'm very  excited and optimistic about AI but I do  have two causes for concern they do not  concern the number of jobs but the first  one is about the quality of jobs and  wages so sometimes the a and sometimes  AI opens the field to more people to  join a job so for instance GPS made it  so that taxi drivers did not have to  memorize maps that opens the field to  more people who could become taxi  drivers and put the wages down push the  wages down and Eric already showed your  chart like this that predicts the drop  in in the middle level jobs so the only  so in my opinion the only way to  mitigate this part polarization is  education and I'd like to observe that  we can imagine short term and long term  solutions in particular for short term  solutions I would like to highlight  bitsource which is a new startup in  Kentucky so big source trains coal  miners to be data miners and guess what  coal miners are not upset about their  old jobs and there are very excited  about the new jobs and the new  opportunities and so the question is how  can we take this model and and  incentivize other industries and other  places to do to do similar programs I  think this will be a great solution for  the short term now in the long term I  think we really have to embrace the idea  that computational thinking is the  fourth pillar for  literacy in the 21st century and to to  introduce mandatory computer science  training from from early age from  kindergarten from first grade I can  imagine that in 2025 the high school  graduates will be equipped with the IT  tools that will allow them to to do this  and I would observe that China South  Korea Japan UK 12 European countries all  have implemented have embraced the idea  of mandatory computer science training  I'm sure that the US will follow and  then we will have great benefits by 2025  now my second cause for concern is what  I call anti intellectualism in our  country and the dumbing down of our  country the the lack of trust and  respect in science and this is something  that we should concern all of us and we  should really ask ourselves what kind of  world do we want to live in now I  believe that technology can be a huge  vector for positive change so how do we  have to step it up to actually change  the world and have people at large have  the public embrace science technology  embrace all these ideas education is one  way but I think we also need to change  how we communicate about AI we need to  inspire people to dream about how their  lives can be better with AI rather than  experiencing fear because of AI and it's  on us to do that so in conclusion I  would say that in one important sense  machines and people are quite similar to  each other in that they can help us or  hurt us depending on the choices we make  we can't stop technology from improving  and changing the world but we can be  proactive and we could think about the  consequences we could think about what  we need to do to ensure that those  changes are for the better I think Eric  Aldous mindfulness with respect to  change so computers and much  Jeanne's do not need to be competitors  especially when they do so much better  working together but to get here we  really need to invest in developing the  field of AI we need to develop the  science of autonomy and the science of  intelligence we need to think broadly  about computer science and computational  thinking as part of literacy and we need  to change how we message about AI to  help people dream rather than get them  scared thank you  [Music]

